  "strategy": "The central idea is to use a two-level hierarchical optimization method that balances exhaustive search and intelligent refinement. The key observation is that the square root of twice the sequence length provides a natural trade-off between the numerator (concerned with convolution) and the denominator (concerned with sum). The algorithm works as follows:\n\n1. Approach and Rationale:\n- Initialize with provided parent and alternative starting points\n- Run breadth-first search on fixed-length candidates\n- Progressively refine best candidates using local optimization\n- For final candidates, employ a thinning strategy by merging complementary structural elements while preserving uniform scaling\n- Energy-based reasoning: \n  * The objective penalizes both high convolution maximum and low mass scaling\n  * Longer sequences coupled with longer decay characteristics yield better trade-offs\n  * The gradient descent step explores efficient scalar trade-offs between these factors\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\n- Let n be the sequence length (default 1, variable)\n- Each coefficient x_i satisfies 0 \u2264 x_i \u2264 1000\n- The objective: f(a) = (2n max(convolve(a,a))) / (sum(a))\u00b2\n- Where convolve is the full mode linear convolution producing 2n-1 coefficients\n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import linear_sum_assignment\nfrom scipy.sparse import lil_matrix\n\ndef propose_candidate(time_limit=None, target=None, parent=None, alpha=None):\n    # Initialize and adapt parameters if target/score provided\n    \n    # Phase 1: Candidate Generation\n    # Use the provided parent if available, otherwise sample from prior art\n    \n    # Phase 2: Search & Optimization\n    # Local refinement options:\n    # Frank-Wolfe step with LP solver\n    def solve_lp(f, time_budget=30):\n        # Set up the linear program to find direction g\n        # Constraints: (convolve(f,g)[k]) \u2264 M for all k\n        \n        # Implementation:\n        # Optimized simplex projection using sparse matrices\n    \n    def fast_convolve(a):\n        # Compute auto-convolution efficiently using FFT\n    \n    # Alternative parameterization and optimization thresholds\n    \n    # Phase 3: Validation & Stopping Conditions\n    # Check at each update if improved state found\n    # Monitor wall-clock time\n    \n    # Phase 4: Thinning Strategy\n    # When all other optimization paths fail, apply a structured thinning mechanism\n    \n    # Extract best solution after time_limit is exhausted\n```\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations:\n- Primary initiative: the provided parent sequence if available\n- Secondary initiatives: periodic patterns with the parent's characteristic period\n- Progressive optimization: Frank-Wolfe followed by stochastic gradient steps\n- Escape mechanisms: introduce periodic diversions when stuck in local optima\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness:\n- Wall-clock time allocation:\n  - 60% for broad exploration (multi-starts)\n  - 20% for local optimization refinement\n  - 20% for final thinning validation\n- Fallback state: min(sum(init_candidate)^2, (2n-1) * current_conv_value)\n- Dimension handling:\n  - Original dimensions preserved unless target forces optimization\n  - Ensure 0 \u2264 x_i \u2264 1000 clip preservation\n\n6. Validation & Return Contract:\n- Final candidate re-validated with full distribution\n- Report only if improved, keep within time limit\n- If wall-clock exceeds 1800 seconds, finish with current state\n\nChoose algorithms that scale with the constructive domain: use thinning strategies that balance decay with uniform prosperity, and use thresholded optimization ensuring genuine gains before exiting time-sensitive environments. The fallback ensures sequences stay viable.\n\nThis approach builds upon expensive autoregressive patterns of the parent but introduces energy-efficient gradient steps. When optimization plateaus, the thinning mechanism bridges the gap by maintaining uniform mass while reducing effective dimensions - a fair trade-off leading to comparable compression qualities.",
